  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
